    {
      "name": "Symbol_CTopLevelWindow_UpdateWindowVisuals_ForcedHighContrast",
      "id": "CTopLevelWindow::UpdateWindowVisuals.forcedHighContrast",
      "bindings": [
        {
          "platform": "arm64",
          "type": "BYTE*",
          "ranges": [
            {
              "symbol_names": [
                "private: long __cdecl CTopLevelWindow::UpdateWindowVisuals(void) __ptr64"
              ],
              "min_inclusive": {
                "build": 22000,
                "revision": 0
              },
              "max_exclusive": null
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "requirement": "optional",
      "kind": "raw",
      "usage": "code_address",
      "notes": "ARM64 anchor for the x64 inlined high-contrast patch. ARM64 UpdateWindowVisuals reads CDesktopManager::IsHighContrastMode inline twice instead of calling it: once to clear a rect and skip the modern background block, once to capture a bool consumed by the later border and background code. Verified on the 22631 ARM64 binary: forcing only the captured-bool branch produces the legacy background fill; forcing the rect-clear branch removes that fill again, and faking the flag for the whole call also reaches GetRoundedBorderColor and the border brush setup, which paints a black ring and adds a border under borderless themes. Forcing that branch alone is not enough: the same high-contrast block keeps the (part - 0x12) <= 3 shadow-part test that x64 also forces out of range, and an unforced test sends the shadow NC area parts down their special path, which drops the window drop shadow. Both decisions are forced here because the search window is anchored on this function; the shadow-part site is the only (value - 0x12) <= 3 comparison followed by b.hi inside it, and the branch is re-encoded as an unconditional branch to its own target. Verified on the same 22631 ARM64 build: restoring the second forced decision brought the shadow back with the modern border disabled. The predicates are matched by byte pattern, so a recompiled uDWM fails the match instead of corrupting code."
    },
